Article 2 Illegally taking compulsory measures against obstruction of litigation includes the following circumstances:
(1) imposing a fine or detention on a person who has not committed any act to obstruct litigation;
(2) imposing a fine exceeding the amount provided by law;
(3) imposing detention exceeding the period provided by law;
(4) repeatedly imposing a fine or detention for the same act of obstructing litigation;
(5) other illegal circumstances.

Article 3 Illegally taking preservation measures includes the following circumstances:
(1) taking preservation measures which should not have been taken according to law;
(2) lifting preservation measures which should not have been lifted according to law, or failing to lift preservation measures which should have been lifted according to law;
(3) taking preservation measures obviously exceeding the scope of the claim, unless the property preserved is indivisible and the person against whom preservation is sought has no other property or such other property is insufficient to secure the realization of the claim;
(4) taking preservation measures against property unrelated to the case in an action for delivery of a specific thing;
(5) illegally preserving property of a person not a party to the case;
(6) failing to perform supervision duties over sealed up, distrained, or frozen property, causing damage or loss of the preserved property;
(7) taking preservation measures against seasonal goods, fresh, perishable, or otherwise unsuitable for long-term storage items, and failing to dispose of them in a timely manner or disposing of them illegally, causing damage or serious depreciation;
(8) taking preservation measures against immovable property or specific movables such as ships, aircraft, and motor vehicles, and failing to notify the relevant registration authority not to register the change of ownership of the preserved property according to law, resulting in the transfer of ownership of the preserved property;
(9) illegally taking behavioral preservation measures;
(10) other illegal circumstances.

Article 4 Illegally taking advance execution measures includes the following circumstances:
(1) executing in advance in violation of the conditions and scope provided by law;
(2) executing in advance beyond the scope of the claim;
(3) other illegal circumstances.

Article 5 Errors in enforcing judgments, rulings, or other effective legal instruments include the following circumstances:
(1) enforcing legal instruments that have not taken effect;
(2) enforcing beyond the amount and scope specified in the effective legal instrument;
(3) intentionally delaying or failing to enforce against property of the person subject to enforcement that has been discovered, resulting in the loss of such property;
(4) failing to resume enforcement when it should be resumed, resulting in the loss of property subject to enforcement;
(5) illegally enforcing property of a person not a party to the case;
(6) illegally delivering the enforced money or property to another party or a person not a party to the case;
(7) illegally taking enforcement measures against mortgaged property, pledged property, or property subject to a lien, thereby preventing the mortgagee, pledgee, or lienholder from realizing their priority right to satisfaction;
(8) failing to perform supervision duties over property sealed up, distrained, or frozen during enforcement, causing damage or loss of the property;
(9) taking enforcement measures against seasonal goods, fresh, perishable, or otherwise unsuitable for long-term storage items, and failing to dispose of them in a timely manner or disposing of them illegally, causing damage or serious depreciation;
(10) failing to auction property subject to enforcement according to law when auction is required, or failing to have it appraised by a property appraisal institution according to law when appraisal is required, and illegally selling it by private sale or offsetting it against a debt;
(11) other erroneous circumstances.

Article 7 The State shall not bear compensation liability under any of the following circumstances:
(1) the circumstances fall within the provisions of Article 105, Paragraph 2 of Article 107, and Article 233 of the Civil Procedure Law;
(2) the applicant for enforcement provides the wrong subject matter of enforcement, unless the People's Court knew the subject matter was wrong but still enforced;
(3) the custodian designated by the People's Court according to law illegally uses, conceals, damages, transfers, or sells by private sale the sealed up, distrained, or frozen property;
(4) the personal act of a functionary of a People's Court that is unrelated to the exercise of functions;
(5) damage caused by force majeure, justifiable defense, or act of rescue;
(6) other circumstances for which the State should not bear compensation liability according to law.

Article 19 A citizen, legal person, or other organization applying for compensation in accordance with Article 38 of the State Compensation Law shall submit the application after the conclusion of the civil action or administrative action procedure or the enforcement procedure, except under the following circumstances:
(1) the People's Court has already revoked the compulsory measures against obstruction of litigation according to law;
(2) the People's Court took compulsory measures against obstruction of litigation, causing physical injury or death to a citizen;
(3) property has been confirmed through litigation procedures according to law not to belong to the person against whom preservation is sought or the person subject to enforcement, and cannot be remedied in the relevant litigation procedure or enforcement procedure;
(4) the effective legal instrument of the People's Court has confirmed that the relevant act is illegal, and cannot be remedied in the relevant litigation procedure or enforcement procedure;
(5) the claimant for compensation has evidence to prove that his or her claim is unrelated to the civil action or administrative action procedure or the enforcement procedure;
(6) other circumstances.
Where the claimant for compensation applies for compensation after the conclusion of the civil action or administrative action procedure or the enforcement procedure in accordance with the preceding paragraph, the period of such procedure shall not be counted in the limitation period for claiming compensation.

Article 20 Where the Compensation Committee of a People's Court tries a judicial compensation case in a civil action or administrative action, the corresponding periods shall not be counted in the trial period under any of the following circumstances:
(1) it is necessary to retrieve case files or other materials from the compensatory obligor, relevant People's Court, or other state organs;
(2) the Compensation Committee of the People's Court commissions expert evaluation or appraisal.
